---
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
---

<CaseLayout
  headline="Turning a customer insights dashboard into a platform other teams could build on."
  intro={[
    'Customer Insights began as an underused Cisco dashboard. I led the research and redesign around the questions account teams asked while preparing for customer meetings. Its user base grew 40% within four months of the redesign.',
    'That growth brought requests from program teams that wanted to add their upgrade logic. I defined where those contributions belonged, what evidence they needed, and which parts of the experience remained under the core team’s control.',
  ]}
  stakeholder="Cisco Ready"
  skills={['Product Discovery', 'Information Architecture', 'Platform Design']}
  year="2020-21 (two phases)"
  role="Lead Product Designer"
  teamLabel="Product, engineering + program teams"
  hero="/assets/cisco/video/opportunity-extension-flow.webm"
  heroVideo
  heroPoster="/assets/cisco/posters/opportunity-extension-flow.jpg"
  heroCaption="Lead Product Designer · Two phases. I redesigned the core product, then set the rules for adding opportunity intelligence."
  theme="cisco"
  previousHref="/work/hitachi-energy/"
  nextHref="/work/panw-ai/"
>
  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>The dashboard users rebuilt in Excel</h2>
    <p>The existing page offered a broad account overview, but it did not match how account managers prepared a customer strategy. To answer basic questions, people moved across internal tools and assembled their own pivot tables.</p>
    <p>The workaround was slow, but the spreadsheets were useful research artifacts. They showed which questions mattered and how people grouped data that the dashboard kept separate.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="asymmetric-grid cisco-evidence-grid" data-reveal>
    <Figure
      src="/assets/cisco/previous-dashboard.png"
      width={3000}
      height={1605}
      alt="Previous Customer Insights dashboard"
      caption="The previous dashboard provided an overview but left the analytical work to the user."
    />
    <Figure
      src="/assets/cisco/participant-pivot-analysis.png"
      width={1709}
      height={3000}
      alt="Participant-created customer analysis pivot tables"
      caption="Account teams’ pivot tables revealed the recurring comparisons hidden behind the request for “everything.”"
    />
  </div>

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Starting with recent decisions</h2>
    <p>I interviewed 12 participants across three countries. Rather than asking them to describe an ideal dashboard, I asked them to reconstruct their two most recent customer meetings.</p>
    <p>Meeting preparation was one of the few times account teams pulled inventory, coverage, contracts, renewals, partner information, and pipeline into one view. The details varied by role, but the same questions kept coming up.</p>
  </section>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/cisco/strategy-sensemaking.png"
    width={3000}
    height={1687}
    alt="Framework connecting customer strategy questions to accessible data"
    caption="I traced each strategy question to its source and checked what the platform could access. This turned the request for “everything” into a finite product model."
  />

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Building the core from repeatable modules</h2>
    <p>I explored visualizations for each recurring question, then looked for structures the product and engineering teams could reuse. Each module paired a headline measure with supporting context and a detailed table or chart.</p>
    <p>Users could follow the same path from overview to evidence in every module. Engineering had fewer structures to maintain.</p>
  </section>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/cisco/visualization-ideation.png"
    width={3000}
    height={1129}
    alt="Board of data visualization concepts"
    caption="Visualization studies tested how the same customer question could move from a quick signal to a detailed analysis."
  />

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Testing two ways to begin</h2>
    <p>An early disagreement led to two opposing concepts. One opened with a catalog of charts and left interpretation to the user. The other used benchmarks to suggest where to look first.</p>
    <p>Participants preferred the guided version. Benchmark color did not work reliably across Cisco’s very different customers, but the test showed that a clear tile hierarchy made the page easier to scan.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="media-grid cisco-concept-grid" data-reveal>
    <Figure
      src="/assets/cisco/exploratory-concept.png"
      width={3000}
      height={2009}
      alt="Exploratory chart-led dashboard concept"
      caption="Concept 01 gave users a catalog of visualizations and left the next step open."
    />
    <Figure
      src="/assets/cisco/guided-concept.png"
      width={3000}
      height={2009}
      alt="Benchmark-guided metric tile dashboard concept"
      caption="Concept 02 used benchmarked tiles to suggest where attention might be useful."
    />
  </div>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/cisco/video/customer-insights-flow.webm"
    poster="/assets/cisco/posters/customer-insights-flow.jpg"
    width={1920}
    height={1080}
    alt="Customer Insights dashboard workflow"
    caption="The redesigned core organized the account around six repeatable questions, then let each module unfold from a headline measure into charts and tables."
    video
    controls
  />

  <section class="case-section reading-column case-transition" data-reveal>
    <h2>Success changed the product’s role</h2>
    <p>As account managers shared Customer Insights with their wider teams, the user base grew 40% in four months. The format was later reused for Global Insights, which applied the same questions across regions rather than one customer at a time.</p>
    <p>Program teams then asked to bring upgrade and buying-program logic into the product. I worked with those teams and engineering to define the terms. Contributions would arrive in phases, could be replaced as programs changed, and had to connect to an existing customer measure.</p>
  </section>

  <figure class="platform-evolution" data-reveal>
    <div class="platform-host">
      <div class="platform-core">
        <span>CORE PRODUCT</span>
        <strong>Customer Insights</strong>
        <p>One stable interaction model for customer context, comparison, and detail.</p>
        <div class="platform-ports" aria-label="Platform rules">
          <span>Placement</span><span>Language</span><span>Release quality</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="platform-contributions" aria-label="Replaceable contributed capabilities">
        <div><span>PROGRAM TEAM 01</span><strong>Upgrade logic</strong><p>Replaceable</p></div>
        <div><span>PROGRAM TEAM 02</span><strong>Buying program</strong><p>Replaceable</p></div>
        <div><span>PROGRAM TEAM 03</span><strong>Renewal logic</strong><p>Replaceable</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figcaption>The core team governed the experience and its connection points. Program teams could update or replace their own logic without redesigning the host product.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section class="case-section reading-column case-transition" data-reveal>
    <h2>Designing a boundary for contributed value</h2>
    <p>Contributing teams owned the eligibility logic and supporting data; the Customer Insights team owned placement, interaction, language, and release quality. That split let another team improve or replace its offer without gaining control of the surrounding experience.</p>
  </section>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/cisco/extension-constraints.png"
    width={3000}
    height={1144}
    alt="Framework for scaling contributed opportunity recommendations"
    caption="The extension contract accounted for phased releases, changing offers, and a required connection to an existing customer measure."
  />

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Prominence did not create relevance</h2>
    <p>I tested the opportunity layer as a panel, list, overlay, and inline module with account-team participants. In the panel and list concepts, people scanned customer data first and often passed over labels built from internal program names. This happened even when the panel occupied a quarter of the screen.</p>
    <p>We stopped asking, “Why aren’t people clicking?” and asked, “What would make this useful now?” The change put the account manager’s goal back at the center of the design.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="media-grid cisco-extension-tests" data-reveal>
    <Figure
      src="/assets/cisco/video/opportunity-panel-test.webm"
      poster="/assets/cisco/posters/opportunity-panel-test.jpg"
      width={1790}
      height={1280}
      alt="Opportunity panel usability concept"
      caption="Panel test: visually prominent, but the program-led labels gave account teams little reason to engage."
      video
      controls
    />
    <Figure
      src="/assets/cisco/video/opportunity-list-test.webm"
      poster="/assets/cisco/posters/opportunity-list-test.jpg"
      width={1790}
      height={1280}
      alt="Opportunity list usability concept"
      caption="List test: the extension remained separate from the analysis, making it easy to scan but difficult to connect with the customer story."
      video
      controls
    />
  </div>

  <section class="case-section reading-column case-transition" data-reveal>
    <h2>Lead with the value, then reveal the program</h2>
    <p>Account managers were working toward quarterly and annual goals. Replacing internal shorthand with a clear action and its potential value made the contribution legible in their terms.</p>
  </section>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/cisco/opportunity-card-copy.png"
    width={3000}
    height={1687}
    alt="Opportunity card before and after copy refinement"
    caption="“Upsell to NCD” became “Upgrade to Next Calendar Day,” paired with the potential value for that customer."
  />

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Use the extension as a lens on the core</h2>
    <p>Each incoming opportunity used a subset of information already present in Customer Insights. We did not need another collection of disconnected tiles.</p>
    <p>Instead, selecting an opportunity changed the lens on familiar charts and tables. The contribution supplied logic; the core product supplied context, comparison, and detail.</p>
  </section>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/cisco/data-lens-model.png"
    width={3000}
    height={853}
    alt="Several opportunities reusing subsets of Customer Insights data"
    caption="Each contributed opportunity reused a different subset of the core data model, allowing specialized views without duplicating the platform."
  />

  <Figure
    src="/assets/cisco/video/opportunity-extension-flow.webm"
    poster="/assets/cisco/posters/opportunity-extension-flow.jpg"
    width={1920}
    height={1080}
    alt="Final contributed opportunity workflow"
    caption="Final flow: choose an opportunity, see its potential value, and inspect the relevant customer data without leaving Customer Insights."
    video
    controls
  />

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Outcome</h2>
    <p>The extension shipped with four opportunity types using the same interaction model. Account teams could open the relevant upgrade logic from current customer data. Contributing teams could update their offers without redesigning Customer Insights.</p>
    <p>We did not have enough time after launch to measure adoption by opportunity type. We did know that four independently owned programs had shipped through one interaction model instead of becoming four separate dashboard sections.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>What I learned</h2>
    <p><strong>The core team needed to control the experience without owning every contribution.</strong></p>
    <p>Finding room for another panel was easy. I had to decide what other teams could add, how it connected to customer data, and how it could change without disrupting the rest of the product.</p>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>
